Remedy → Mongo → PWA Real-Time Sync: Implementation Spec
Sep 19, 2026 · @Ashok Naik
Handoff specification for a coding agent. Node.js only — no changes to BMC Remedy, no DBA-owned tooling.
1. Context and goal
What exists today. ENBD tracks P1–P4 incidents in BMC Remedy, backed by an old Oracle database. A Node.js service reads from Oracle, writes to MongoDB, and serves a PWA. The current sync runs hourly.
Problem with the current design. Hourly polling gives neither freshness nor safety. A P1 raised at 10:01 surfaces at 11:00, and a strict modified > watermark comparison can permanently drop tickets whose Remedy transaction commits after the poll ran. The second failure is silent — no error, no gap in the logs.
What we are building. A near-real-time P1/P2 pipeline that lives entirely in Node.js. No Remedy filter changes, no GoldenGate, no Debezium, no DBA-owned tooling, no schema changes in Remedy. The only things asked of the Remedy/Oracle owners are a read-only account and confirmation of an index.
Definition of done.
• A P1 or P2 created or updated in Remedy is visible in the PWA within 60 seconds (target: 30s median).
• A P1 reaches an on-call engineer whose PWA is closed or backgrounded, via Web Push.
• Oracle load is lower than the current hourly job, measured in logical reads per hour.
• Zero silent drops: no ticket meeting the P1/P2 filter can fail to appear without an alert firing.
• The pipeline survives a pod restart, a Mongo failover, and an Oracle outage without data loss or duplicate notifications.
2. Architecture
Oracle (BMC Remedy)
   │  ① adaptive watermark poll — node-oracledb, read-only user, 30–60s
   ▼
Ingestion worker (Node)  ← only the leader polls; lease held in Mongo
   │  ② idempotent bulkWrite upsert, monotonic guard
   ▼
MongoDB (replica set)
   │  ③ change stream, filtered to P1/P2, resume token persisted
   ▼
API pods (Node, N replicas)
   │  ④ SSE per user + ⑤ Web Push for P1
   ▼
PWA
Why this shape. Mongo change streams replace a message broker, so there is no Kafka or Redis to operate. SSE replaces WebSockets because the incident feed is one-way and SSE reconnects and replays natively. The leader lease keeps N pods from becoming N pollers against Remedy's database.
