4. Component 1 — the Oracle watermark poller
What a watermark is
A single integer stored in Mongo, not Oracle. Oracle has no concept of it. It holds the highest C6 (Modified Date) value the poller has ever seen. Each cycle asks Oracle only for rows at or above that mark, so a typical poll returns zero rows and costs almost nothing. Frequency controls freshness; the watermark controls cost. They are independent knobs and the design needs both.
Four rules that must not be broken
These exist because each one, violated, causes a silent loss of a P1. Treat them as non-negotiable and cover each with a test in section 13.
1. Query >= watermark - OVERLAP_SEC, never > watermark. Remedy stamps Modified Date when the field is set, but the transaction commits later. A row stamped 10:00:00 that commits at 10:00:04 is invisible to a poll at 10:00:02 that then stores a watermark of 10:00:01 — and a strict > excludes it forever. The overlap re-reads that window every cycle.
2. Derive the new watermark from MAX(C6) of rows Oracle returned — never Date.now(). The Node host clock and the Oracle host clock are different clocks. Using local time manufactures the same gap by hand.
3. Persist the watermark only after the Mongo writes commit. Writing it first turns a crash into permanent data loss: the rows were never stored but the bookmark says they were.
4. If the result set is empty, leave the watermark untouched. Do not advance it to "now" — rows may be mid-commit in that window.
Reference implementation
const OVERLAP_SEC = 300; // must exceed observed commit lag (section 3)

async function pollOnce(conn, db) {
  const state = db.collection('sync_state');
  let doc = await state.findOne({ _id: 'hpd_helpdesk' });

  // Cold start: no bookmark yet
  if (!doc) {
    const from = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) - (BACKFILL_DAYS * 86400);
    await state.insertOne({ _id: 'hpd_helpdesk', watermark: from });
    doc = { watermark: from };
  }

  const lower = doc.watermark - OVERLAP_SEC;

  const rs = await conn.execute(
    `SELECT C1, C6, C7, C1000000164, C1000000000, C1000000063, C1000000217
       FROM "HPD:Help Desk"
      WHERE C6 >= :lower
        AND C1000000164 IN (0, 1)`,
    { lower },
    { outFormat: oracledb.OUT_FORMAT_OBJECT, fetchArraySize: 500 }
  );

  if (rs.rows.length === 0) return { rows: 0 };   // rule 4

  await upsertIncidents(db, rs.rows);              // must fully commit first

  const maxTs = rs.rows.reduce((m, r) => Math.max(m, r.C6), doc.watermark); // rule 2
  await state.updateOne(                            // rule 3 — last
    { _id: 'hpd_helpdesk' },
    { $set: { watermark: maxTs, lastPollAt: new Date(), lastRows: rs.rows.length } }
  );

  return { rows: rs.rows.length, watermark: maxTs };
}
Oracle client settings
• Pool poolMin: 1, poolMax: 2. The poller runs one query at a time and needs one connection; the spare covers the reconciler. A large pool adds idle sessions to someone else's production database for no benefit. If a bigger pool ever looks necessary, the query is slow and needs an index — not more connections.
• Bind variables always (:lower), so Oracle reuses the cursor instead of flooding the shared pool with hard parses.
• callTimeout of ~15s, so a stuck query cannot pile up connections.
• Project only the columns needed. No SELECT *.
• oracledb.thin mode unless CQN is added later (thick mode required for CQN).
