If pursued at all, use it only to wake the poll loop early. The system must be fully correct with CQN disabled.
5. Component 2 — leader election
If every pod polls, N pods means N times the Oracle load, which is exactly what this design set out to avoid. One leader polls; the rest stand by and take over within 30 seconds of a leader dying.
A Mongo lease is sufficient. Do not add ZooKeeper, etcd, or Redis for this.
const LEASE_MS = 30_000;

async function tryAcquireLease(db) {
  const now = new Date();
  const res = await db.collection('locks').findOneAndUpdate(
    { _id: 'oracle-poller',
      $or: [{ expiresAt: { $lt: now } }, { owner: POD_ID }] },
    { $set: { owner: POD_ID, expiresAt: new Date(Date.now() + LEASE_MS) } },
    { upsert: true, returnDocument: 'after' }
  );
  return res?.owner === POD_ID;
}
Requirements.
• Renew the lease every LEASE_MS / 3. A leader that fails to renew must stop polling immediately, before the lease expires, so two pods never poll at once.
• The $or clause lets the current holder renew while blocking everyone else.
• Handle the upsert duplicate-key race: on E11000, treat it as losing the election and retry next tick.
• Expose leadership state on /health so it is visible which pod is active.
• On graceful shutdown (SIGTERM), release the lease by setting expiresAt to now, so failover is instant rather than 30 seconds.
