The idempotent upsert
The overlap window means the same row is read several times. That must be harmless. The $lt guard makes a re-read a no-op and, more importantly, stops an out-of-order arrival from overwriting newer state.
async function upsertIncidents(db, rows) {
  const ops = rows.map(r => ({
    updateOne: {
      filter: { _id: r.C1, sourceTs: { $lt: r.C6 } },
      update: { $set: mapIncident(r), $setOnInsert: { firstSeenAt: new Date() } },
      upsert: true
    }
  }));
  await db.collection('incidents').bulkWrite(ops, { ordered: false });
}
Wrinkle the agent must handle. An upsert whose filter includes sourceTs: { $lt: ... } will attempt an insert when the document exists but the guard fails, producing a duplicate-key error on _id. With ordered: false the rest of the batch still applies. Catch the BulkWriteError, filter out code 11000, and treat those as successful no-ops. Any other error must fail the cycle so the watermark does not advance.
A replica set is required — change streams do not work against a standalone mongod. Confirm the target environment before starting.
7. Component 4 — change stream to SSE fan-out
Why SSE and not WebSockets
The incident feed is one-way: server to client, and the client never pushes back over the same channel. SSE runs over ordinary HTTP, so it passes through the bank's proxies and load balancers with no upgrade handling, reconnects automatically, and supports replay natively via Last-Event-ID. WebSockets would add heartbeats, reconnection code, sticky routing, and LB configuration for a capability nothing here uses.
If the PWA later needs to write back to Remedy (acknowledge, assign, add a work note), that is a REST call, not a reason to switch transports.
Each API pod watches the collection
const pipeline = [{
  $match: {
    'fullDocument.priority': { $in: [0, 1] },
    operationType: { $in: ['insert', 'update', 'replace'] }
  }
}];

const stream = db.collection('incidents').watch(pipeline, {
  fullDocument: 'updateLookup',
  resumeAfter: savedToken   // omit on first ever start
});

for await (const change of stream) {
  enqueue(change.fullDocument);
  await saveResumeToken(change._id);
}
Resume handling. Persist the token after each batch. On reconnect, pass resumeAfter. If Mongo rejects the token because the oplog has rolled past it, fall back to a full reload of open P1/P2 documents and log it as a degraded restart.
Batching
Do not emit one SSE frame per change. Coalesce into a 250ms window and send one frame containing the batch. A bulk update in Remedy — a mass reprioritisation, a bulk close — can produce hundreds of changes in a second, and per-row frames will saturate slow mobile connections. Discord's engineering write-ups make the same point: they built a batched event dispatcher specifically to cut fan-out cost.
Also: do not hold the full incident set in memory in every pod. Discord's first relay design kept a full copy of the member list in each relay and it wasted large amounts of memory and slowed startup; the fix was giving each relay only what it used. Each pod here should hold only the connections it serves and the deltas passing through.
SSE endpoint
app.get('/api/stream', authenticate, (req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, {
    'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream',
    'Cache-Control': 'no-cache, no-transform',
    'Connection': 'keep-alive',
    'X-Accel-Buffering': 'no'        // required behind nginx
  });

  const since = req.headers['last-event-id'];
  if (since) replaySince(res, since);

  registry.add(req.user.id, res);
  const hb = setInterval(() => res.write(': hb\n\n'), 15000);
  req.on('close', () => { clearInterval(hb); registry.remove(req.user.id, res); });
});
Requirements.
• Every event carries a monotonic id: so Last-Event-ID replay works. Use the incident's sourceTs plus a sequence, or a server-side counter persisted in Mongo.
• Heartbeat comment every 15s, or intermediate proxies will close idle connections.
• X-Accel-Buffering: no and no-transform, or nginx buffers the stream and nothing arrives until the buffer fills.
• Cap connections per user (2–3) so a reconnect loop cannot exhaust file descriptors.
• Backpressure: if res.write() returns false, drop the oldest queued events for that client rather than growing the buffer without bound.
• On pod shutdown, send a retry: hint and close cleanly so clients reconnect on a stagger rather than all at once.
8. Component 5 — Web Push for P1
SSE only reaches an open tab. The actual business requirement behind "real-time P1 alerting" is reaching an on-call engineer whose phone is in their pocket. That needs the Web Push API and a service worker.
Scope. P1 only. P2 goes to the SSE feed and the in-app badge. Pushing every P2 trains people to dismiss notifications, and then the P1 gets dismissed too.
Implementation.
• web-push npm package, VAPID keys in the secret store (never in the repo, never in client bundles).
• Service worker handles push and notificationclick; clicking opens the PWA deep-linked to that incident.
• Subscriptions stored per user; a 410 Gone or 404 from the push service means the subscription is dead — delete it rather than retrying.
• Track failureCount and prune after repeated failures.
Deduplication is mandatory. A single P1 can be modified a dozen times in its first ten minutes. Push once per incident on the transition into P1 (or on creation at P1), then suppress further pushes for that incident for a cooldown window (default 30 minutes), unless the status changes to resolved. Without this the pipeline becomes a pager storm and the feature gets turned off.
Keep the payload minimal: incident number, priority, a short summary, and the deep link. Push payloads travel through Google's and Mozilla's infrastructure — see section 12.
9. Component 6 — reconciliation sweep
The watermark poll has two blind spots it can never cover on its own, and both are cheap to close because the open P1/P2 set is small — typically a few hundred rows.
Blind spot 1: hard deletes. A delta on a modified-date column cannot observe a row that was physically removed. Deleted or merged tickets would linger in Mongo forever.
Blind spot 2: edits older than the overlap window. A back-dated correction, a data fix, or a restore can land with a modified date well below watermark - OVERLAP_SEC. The overlap re-scan will not see it.
The sweep. Every 15 minutes, on the leader:
// 1. Pull the full current key set — IDs and timestamps only, no wide columns
const rs = await conn.execute(
  `SELECT C1, C6 FROM "HPD:Help Desk"
    WHERE C1000000164 IN (0,1) AND C7 IN (0,1,2,3)`   // open statuses
);

// 2. Mark everything seen
await db.collection('incidents').updateMany(
  { _id: { $in: rs.rows.map(r => r.C1) } },
  { $set: { seenInSweepAt: now } }
);

// 3. Anything open in Mongo but absent from Oracle is stale — reap it
const stale = await db.collection('incidents').find({
  status: { $in: OPEN_STATUSES },
  seenInSweepAt: { $lt: new Date(now - 2 * SWEEP_INTERVAL) }
}).toArray();

// 4. Any row whose Oracle C6 differs from Mongo sourceTs was missed — re-fetch it
Step 4 is the important one: it is the only mechanism that catches a genuinely dropped ticket. Count the mismatches and emit them as a metric. A non-zero value means the watermark logic has a hole — most likely OVERLAP_SEC is smaller than the real commit lag. This metric is the early warning that the pipeline is losing data, and it should alert.
The sweep query touches two columns across a few hundred rows. It is not a full-table scan and does not need to run outside business hours.
