12. Security and bank constraints
• Read-only Oracle account, scoped to the incident form's view only. No SELECT ANY TABLE, no write grants. The application must be incapable of modifying Remedy.
• Credentials from the secret store (Vault, K8s secrets, or whatever ENBD standardises on). Never in the repo, config files, or container images.
• TLS to Oracle, and mTLS between services if the platform supports it.
• Field minimisation. Pull only what the dashboard renders. Remedy incident records can contain customer names, account numbers, and free-text descriptions with PII embedded. If the PWA shows a summary and an assignment group, do not sync the full work-log detail. Every field added is a field that now lives in a second datastore and a push payload.
• Web Push payloads leave the bank's perimeter via Google's and Mozilla's push services. They are encrypted end-to-end, but the endpoint metadata is not. Keep payloads to an incident number and a generic title; fetch detail from the API after the user opens the app. Get this reviewed by infosec before launch, as it is the most likely point of objection.
• Authentication on the SSE endpoint with the same SSO the PWA already uses. Note that browsers' native EventSource cannot send custom headers — use a cookie-based session, or the eventsource polyfill if bearer tokens are mandated.
• Authorisation. If incident visibility is restricted by support group, filter server-side per connection. Do not fan out everything and filter in the client.
• Audit logging of every Oracle query the service makes, if ENBD requires it for production database access.
• Retention. Agree how long incident copies persist in Mongo. A TTL index on closed incidents is likely required by data-governance policy.
13. Acceptance criteria and tests
Correctness tests (the ones that matter)
These must be automated against a seeded Oracle fixture or a mock. Each maps to a failure that is silent in production.
1. Late commit is not lost. Seed a row with C6 = T, run a poll that stores watermark T + 1, then make the row visible. The next poll must return it. (Fails if the query uses > instead of >= watermark - OVERLAP_SEC.)
2. Re-reading the overlap does not duplicate. Run three consecutive polls with no source changes. incidents document count and lastSyncedAt values must be unchanged after the first.
3. Out-of-order arrival does not regress state. Apply a row with sourceTs = 100, then one with sourceTs = 50. The document must retain the sourceTs = 100 content.
4. Crash between upsert and watermark write loses nothing. Kill the process after upsertIncidents resolves but before the sync_state update. On restart the same window is reprocessed and the outcome is identical.
5. Empty result does not advance the watermark. Poll with no matching rows; sync_state.watermark must be byte-identical afterwards.
6. Watermark never derives from local time. Skew the test container's clock 10 minutes ahead of the Oracle fixture. Behaviour must be unchanged.
7. Two pods, one poller. Start three instances against one Mongo. Assert exactly one Oracle session and one poll per interval.
8. Leader failover. SIGKILL the leader. Another pod must poll within LEASE_MS, with no duplicate or skipped window.
9. Change-stream resume. Restart an API pod mid-flow; events produced during the gap must still reach a connected client.
10. SSE replay. Disconnect a client, produce three events, reconnect with Last-Event-ID. All three arrive, in order, once.
11. Push dedup. Update one P1 twenty times in a minute. Exactly one push is sent.
12. Reconciler catches a planted drop. Manually delete a document from Mongo that exists in Oracle. The sweep must detect it, re-fetch it, and increment reconcile_mismatch_count.
Performance gate
Before production, capture a 24-hour comparison against the baseline from section 3: logical reads per hour, average and p99 poll duration, and peak concurrent Oracle sessions. The new design must show fewer logical reads per hour than the existing hourly job. If it does not, the index is not being used — stop and fix that before deploying.
End-to-end gate
Raise a real P1 in a Remedy test instance. Measure Remedy submit time to PWA render and to push delivery. Both must land under 60 seconds, with a median near 30.
